---

import AboutArticle from "../../components/AboutArticle.astro";
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import JSONProbe from "../../assets/json-temp.jpg";
import { getAboutPage } from "../../lib/aboutPages";
import { getAboutPhoto } from "../../lib/aboutPhotos";

const page = getAboutPage("python-feeds")!;
const hero = getAboutPhoto("ethernet-cable");
---
<AboutArticle
  slug={page.slug}
  title={page.title}
  description={page.description}
  heroImage={hero.image}
  heroAlt={hero.alt}
  heroCaption={hero.caption}
  heroCredit={hero.credit}
  heroCreditHref={hero.sourceUrl}
>
  <p>
    Microcontrollers excel at reading probes; Python excels at caching, authentication, and serving
    stable HTTPS endpoints. The fast-api-relay project sits between Arduino JSON and the Astro site,
    normalizing responses and reducing load on fragile embedded stacks.
  </p>

  <h2>Why a relay layer helps</h2>
  <p>
    Direct browser fetches to a LAN IP address break when DNS changes, certificates expire, or the
    home uplink drops. A relay on a VPS or cloud worker presents one consistent URL, adds Redis caching
    so dashboards do not hammer the probe, and can aggregate multiple sites if you expand beyond a single property.
  </p>

  <h2>FastAPI responsibilities</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Accept upstream fetches from the Arduino or local network agent on a schedule.</li>
    <li>Store the latest payload in Redis with a short TTL.</li>
    <li>Serve <code>GET /...</code> responses instantly from cache for the website.</li>
    <li>Log stale data when upstream stops updating: useful for alerting.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Example consumer fetch</h2>
  <p>
    The Astro site’s fetch layer requests your configured HTTPS URL, parses the <code>temp</code> object
    (or auto-detects SenML / Home Assistant REST JSON when that root is missing),
    and maps keys to dashboard probes. The same URL can power Node scripts, Grafana JSON plugins, or
    Home Assistant REST sensors.
  </p>

  <figure class="about-figure">
    <Image
      src={JSONProbe}
      alt="Browser JSON viewer showing temp probe keys 0, 1, and avg with Fahrenheit Celsius and humidity"
      widths={[480, 768, 1024]}
      sizes="(max-width: 768px) 100vw, 640px"
      class="w-full h-auto rounded-lg"
    />
    <figcaption>Example relay payload shape the website expects after caching upstream Arduino JSON.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <pre><code>{`# Periodic upstream pull (cron or asyncio task)
import httpx

UPSTREAM = "http://192.168.1.50/temp.json"
response = httpx.get(UPSTREAM, timeout=5.0)
response.raise_for_status()
cache.set("garage:latest", response.text)`}</code></pre>

  <h2>Operational scripts</h2>
  <p>
    Beyond the API, small Python utilities validate JSON schema, backfill CSV archives, or compare probe
    averages against OpenWeather forecasts. Because probes expose plain JSON, you can prototype analytics
    in a notebook before promoting logic into production services.
  </p>

  <h2>Repository reference</h2>
  <p>
    Implementation details, Redis configuration, and deployment notes for the relay are maintained in
    <a class="text-link" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" href="https://github.com/doodersrage/fast-api-relay">fast-api-relay</a>.
    Configure the public relay URL in your
    <a class="text-link" href="/dashboard/devices">temperature feed settings</a> after signing in.
  </p>

  <h2>Uno Ethernet: HTTP→HTTPS for push ingest</h2>
  <p>
    A W5100 shield cannot POST to <code>https://thermaltrace.dev</code>. The stdlib relay
    <a
      class="text-link"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noreferrer"
      href="https://github.com/doodersrage/thermaltrace/blob/main/sketches/relay/push_https_forward.py"
    ><code>sketches/relay/push_https_forward.py</code></a>
    listens for HTTP from the Arduino and forwards the same path and body over HTTPS.
    Pair it with
    <a
      class="text-link"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noreferrer"
      href="https://github.com/doodersrage/thermaltrace/tree/main/sketches/arduino/ethernet_dht22_ingest"
    ><code>ethernet_dht22_ingest</code></a>
    (DHT22 on A4/A5, classic <code>temp</code> JSON).
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`python3 sketches/relay/push_https_forward.py --listen 0.0.0.0:8080 \\
  --upstream https://thermaltrace.dev`}</code></pre>
</AboutArticle>
